# Sizing profile for the PyG assembly leg (--mode pyg_only). Source it BEFORE the
# variables that identify your cluster:
#
#   . bin/profiles/pyg-assembly.env
#   . ~/my-cluster.env          # master URL, driver host, bucket paths -- NOT tracked
#   bin/submit_spark_job.sh --mode pyg_only ...
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT large-run.env: the two legs pull in opposite directions. Enrichment
# is executor-heavy -- hundreds of millions of triples joined and aggregated out on the
# cluster, nothing large on the driver. Assembly is the reverse. The executors only sort
# and join; the HeteroData itself is built in the driver's own memory, and it is big.
#
# WHAT THE DRIVER HAS TO HOLD, measured on the 2026-08 graph (10,348,460 nodes across
# 72 types, 76,764,031 edges):
#
#   node features   10,348,460 x 1024 x 4B  = 42.4 GiB   <- the dominant term
#   edge indices    76,764,031 x 2 x 8B     =  1.2 GiB
#   edge features   76,764,031 x 32 x 4B    =  9.8 GiB   (featurized types only)
#                                             --------
#                                              53.4 GiB  held at the same time, plus
#                                                        whatever torch.save copies
#
# Almost none of that is JVM heap. numpy owns it, outside -Xmx, so DRIVER_MEMORY does
# not size it -- what sizes it is how much of the host is left unclaimed.
#
# The cluster puts ONE executor on each host, and one of those hosts also carries the
# driver. So the budget that matters is that host's, and only its own executor is in it:
#
#   executor heap            16
#   executor JVM overhead     2
#   RMM pool                  8.8   (unified memory -- the pool is host RAM)
#   pinned pool               2
#   driver heap               8
#   master + worker daemons   2
#   OS and page cache         6
#                          ------
#                            44.8   leaving ~76 GiB of 121 for the driver's tensors
#
# THE POOL LINE IS NOT max_fraction x host RAM. RAPIDS sizes it as
#
#   pool = (gpu.free - reserve) x allocFraction     capped at gpu.total x maxAllocFraction
#
# so it is the ALLOC fraction that decides the size, against memory FREE at the moment
# the executor starts -- not the cap, and not the whole host. Here that is
# 0.08 x ~110 GiB free = 8.8 GiB; the 0.12 cap would allow 14.5 and never binds. An
# earlier version of this line read "0.12 x 121 = 14.5" and overstated the executor
# side by 5.7 GiB. Measured pools on the 2026-08-26 run were 8,609.8, 8,852.1 and
# 8,964.5 MB, which is the alloc fraction, not the cap.
#
# Sizing against FREE memory is also why a replacement executor is not guaranteed the
# pool its predecessor had -- see RAPIDS_GPU_MIN_ALLOC_FRACTION below.
#
# against 45 GiB for a leg with no edge features and ~55 GiB for one with them. An
# earlier draft of this file said 24g and 0.15, which left three gigabytes of margin --
# not margin, just a slower way to reach the same OOM.
#
# large-run.env spends 94 GiB of the same host on the executor side (48 heap + 42 pool
# + 4 pinned). Assembly cannot fit underneath that, which is the arithmetic behind the
# 2026-08-25 host OOM: the driver asked for memory the profile had already promised
# elsewhere, the box went to swap, and the kernel killed the executor -- the largest
# process, though not the one doing the asking.
#
# THE BIGGEST LEVER IS NOT IN THIS FILE. vector_dim drives the dominant term: at 256 the
# node features are 10.6 GiB instead of 42.4 and the leg fits with room to spare. It is
# an experiment variable, though, set per run in pyg_config -- not a sizing knob.

# 16g, not 48g: the assembly leg's Spark work is a sort and two joins over 76.7M rows of
# three integer columns, which is small next to what enrichment does. Cached frames use
# MEMORY_AND_DISK, so a tight executor spills rather than fails. Every gigabyte not spent
# here is a gigabyte the driver's tensors can use.
export EXECUTOR_MEMORY=16g

# Sizes the JVM only. The driver's real appetite is numpy, which this does not bound;
# 8g is comfortable for the Arrow batches and the plan bookkeeping of a ~40-stage job.
# Raising it takes memory away from the tensors it is meant to protect.
export DRIVER_MEMORY=8g

# On a unified-memory GPU the RMM pool is host RAM, so it competes with the driver
# directly. 0.08 of free memory is ~8.8 GiB here; 0.12 is the cap, and does not bind.
#
# "Expect some retries" used to follow, and it was too generous. Node feature assembly
# logged 19,946 to 29,515 "maximum pool size exceeded" lines per leg on 2026-08-26, with
# 600-1,400 rounds of cached-block eviction behind them, and one executor wedged inside
# that eviction for ten minutes and was killed by NETWORK_TIMEOUT. The retries are
# non-fatal one at a time and expensive in bulk. What made them bulk was the assembly
# holding two copies of the same 1.5B sparse entries -- fixed in feature_extractor.py --
# not the size of the pool, so the fractions stay where they are.
export RAPIDS_GPU_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.08
export RAPIDS_GPU_MAX_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.12

# Refuse an executor whose pool comes up unusably small instead of letting it die on
# its first allocation. This is a fraction of TOTAL memory, unlike the alloc fraction
# above, so 0.02 is ~2.4 GiB against a healthy ~8.8.
#
# The case it catches: on 2026-08-26 an executor was killed at 23:48:17 and its
# replacement started one second later, while the dying one still held the card. It
# sized 0.08 against what little was free and came up with a 67.8 MB pool, then died on
# java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: std::bad_alloc -- which names nothing. With a floor,
# RAPIDS refuses at startup and says the free memory, the fraction and the reserve it
# used, and the worker starts another executor once the card is actually released.
#
# Deliberately well below a healthy pool. The next executor on that run came up with
# 2,637.8 MB and finished the leg on roughly a third of the pool; a floor high enough
# to refuse that would also refuse an executor starting on a legitimately busy host,
# and repeated refusals hit spark.deploy.maxExecutorRetries and kill the run. A
# degraded pool is a thing to report, not to refuse -- see the run harness.
export RAPIDS_GPU_MIN_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.02

# Eight task slots per executor, sixteen across the two, each dividing the pool above
# eight ways -- roughly 1.1 GiB a slot.
#
# This was 0.25, four slots, until 2026-09-05. Two runs a day apart decided it: the same
# day of source data, byte-identical enriched Parquet going in, .pt files identical in
# size coming out, and this line the only difference between them. Per leg, the one with
# no edge features and then the 1024d one:
#
#                                       0.25               0.125
#   concurrency, time-weighted      7.27 / 7.38       14.39 / 14.44
#   busy wall spent at the cap       82% / 84%          82% / 81%
#   leg wall                      50.7 / 73.9 min    41.4 / 61.8 min
#   "maximum pool size exceeded" 34,571 / 45,912   100,024 / 132,419
#
# The slots are the cap and they stay saturated at both settings -- doubling them
# doubled the concurrency actually achieved, almost exactly. What did not follow was the
# wall, and that is the reason to stop here rather than halve again. Multiply the first
# two rows out and the total task time ROSE, 344.6 to 549.7 task-minutes on one leg and
# 518.1 to 766.8 on the other, to build the same graph. Roughly half the new parallelism
# went into contention, and the pool retries nearly tripled paying for it.
#
# 0.0625 was rejected on that curve rather than tried. It divides the same pool sixteen
# ways, about 550 MiB a slot, when the last halving already cost 2.9x the pool retries
# to return 17% of the wall. It is not the cores that stop it -- sixteen slots still fit
# a host's cores. If it is ever worth another look, RAPIDS_GPU_ALLOC_FRACTION has to
# rise with it so the per-slot share holds, and that memory comes straight out of the
# driver's tensors, which is what this profile exists to protect.
export GPU_PER_TASK=0.125

# Host memory staged for host<->device transfer, divided among the task slots above --
# eight ways at the GPU_PER_TASK set here, not four. Left at 2G because that is what the
# 2026-09-05 run held at eight slots; it moves with the slot count, so check it here
# before changing that line.
export RAPIDS_PINNED_POOL=2G

# Same reasoning as large-run.env: a busy executor can go quiet longer than the stock
# 120s without being dead, and evicting one destroys checkpoint blocks that have no
# replica.
export NETWORK_TIMEOUT=600s
export EXECUTOR_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=30s

# Move collected columns as Arrow buffers instead of one object per row. Spark's default
# is off. This is the difference between ~20 bytes an edge and a few hundred, and it is
# what keeps the chunked edge collect inside its budget.
export PYSPARK_ARROW_ENABLED=true

# Spark's own default is 1g. The chunk budget in edge_mapper.py bounds one collect at
# roughly 400 MB, so this is headroom -- if a run approaches it, lower the budget rather
# than raise this.
export DRIVER_MAX_RESULT_SIZE=8g

# ALL renders a plan per execution and buries the log. Turn it on deliberately when
# diagnosing GPU fallback -- it is the only source for the REASON a fallback happened.
# With NONE, multi_experiment.ipynb reports GPU placement from the Spark event log, where
# every operator the plugin claimed is named Gpu*, rather than calling it "NO GPU".
export RAPIDS_EXPLAIN=NONE
